WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.170
OK, take a look at that palm tree right over
there.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.780
That’s not a California palm tree, it’s
a Florida palm tree.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.010
And I’m currently in a city that had more
than 30 movie studios and made 100s of films.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:22.800
This 
city was locked in a fight with Hollywood

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.480
and if it won, it could have changed our culture.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:39.090
But this gutted old movie studio is just about
all that’s left of it today.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.289
And the fact that this city lost says something
about early movies and how hard it is for

00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:46.870
cities to embrace change.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.590
But if things had gone just a little differently,
Jacksonville, Florida, might have been the

00:00:51.590 --> 00:01:03.300
world’s movie capital.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.490
This is a typical movie studio around 1910.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.490
Notice the ceiling?

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.900
It’s glass covered by thin paper.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.760
Physical film strips at the time needed so
much light that the studios were like greenhouses,

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.860
and pricey artificial lights were just starting
to get good in the 1910s.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.260
Most early American silent movies were made
in New York — and this lighting requirement

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.280
created some problems.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.290
It was just one of the reasons movie studios
felt “the call of jacksonville.”

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:35.150
New York’s light didn’t compare to “the
rays of the sun” in warmer climates, and

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.860
this was especially important with primitive
film.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.290
Cold weather also hurt cameras, and cameramen
claimed it caused static that produced

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.610
cracks and scratches on the film.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.450
Location was limited as well.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:52.270
New York could play a city, but producers
wanted to be near beaches and jungles.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:58.060
Jacksonville and LA both did that - but Jacksonville
was a relatively short 26 hour train ride

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.140
from New York.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.510
So it became a winter hub.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:13.410
The Vim headquarters in New York wanted a
presence in Jacksonville, so they set up an

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.160
outpost, and the famous Thanhouser Film Company
did the same.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.969
Massive new studios were built.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:27.659
This is the former home of Eagle Studios — a
Jacksonville native studio —in 1916, where

00:02:27.659 --> 00:02:33.310
they shot movies like this one: A Bathtub
Elopement, featuring goofy silent film comedian

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.489
Marcel Perez.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.459
This is the production office.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:44.090
This is the safe where they stored flammable
film.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.530
And this is the on-lot generator.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.769
So why is it empty today?

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:55.620
OK, this is almost it.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.459
Imagine this happening in your city.

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:05.510
Right not I’m at Monroe and Davis in Jacksonville,
where a mob of more than 1,000 extras almost

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.680
destroyed a saloon while shooting the movie
“The Clarion.”

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.560
Like, totally destroyed it.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.700
OK, do I look like that guy?
“ARGH!”

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.000
This happened more than once.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.129
Here’s a crowd chasing a baby carriage down
the street in downtown Jacksonville.

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:28.180
It held a very large “baby.”

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.050
By the mid-1910s, Jacksonville residents were
starting to get sick of movies overrunning

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:39.069
their town.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:44.489
Jacksonville had extended a “genuine welcome”
to film, like Vim Comedy and Eagle Film.

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:49.859
The mayor was a film booster who thought film
would bring a “new era to Jacksonville.”

00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:55.219
He had the support of every production company
in Jacksonville, and the city was set to be

00:03:55.219 --> 00:03:56.640
a hub.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.650
He was up for election in 1917, a year after
that big riot during the shooting of “The

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:04.650
Clarion.”

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.260
Businessmen were behind the mayor and he asked
the people of Jacksonville to vote for him

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.689
and support the motion picture film companies
he brought in.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:16.639
He lost.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:24.260
It was a vote against corruption, but also
against the movies running wild through Jacksonville.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:29.190
During this time, Hollywood had grown a lot,
despite being further away from New York.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.910
Mismanagement of companies in Jacksonville,
World War I, and an influenza outbreak didn’t

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.730
help the Florida city.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.600
There was a lot of bad luck.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.960
After the 1917 election, a lot of studios
closed up shop in Florida.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.730
The city had, in effect, voted against a future
in movies.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:53.020
In the meantime, hit movies, like those made
by legendary director DW Griffith, had made

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.570
Hollywood a home for film..

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.480
Hollywood was ready to be a movie town.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.060
Jacksonville couldn’t make the jump.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.610
Jacksonville shows us an alternate universe
for movies.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:13.390
One where different comedy teams became legendary,
where the duo of Laurel and Hardy was “Plump

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:14.620
and Runt” instead.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.980
And there are other reasons too, beyond movie
stars.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.820
That sign up top should say Norman Laboratories.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.220
After Eagle Studios closed, a producer named
Richard Norman bought it.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:33.290
While Hollywood made movies featuring actors
in blackface, Norman Studios made films like

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.940
the Flying Ace, starring heroic black characters,
played by black actors.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:44.350
It was one of a few film studios that focused
on black audiences and black characters, and

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.120
it sat outside the Hollywood system.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.550
It wasn’t the mainstream.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:55.000
If things had gone a little differently in
Jacksonville, our culture could have been

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.380
shaped by an entirely different set of stories.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:05.130
But instead, all we have are a few restored
films and some buildings that are waiting

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.390
for the same.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:17.040
Alright, that’s it for this episode in our
series about big changes to movies that came

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.610
from outside Hollywood.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:23.400
A lot of places have actually started to surpass
Hollywood — there’s Nollywood, there’s

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.620
Bollywood, there’s Y’allywood — these
are all real, Google them.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.120
So I wanna know if you think Hollywood will
endure.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.860
Let me know in the comments.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.070
I also want to give a shout out to almost
Hollywood by Blair Miller.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.780
All those library scenes where I’m looking
up stuff on microfiche, I’m basically looking

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:44.280
up footnotes in this book and finding them
in the original newspaper.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.960
So if you want to nerd out a lot more on the
history of Jacksonville, Florida, get into

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.900
the specifics, this is the book for you.

